  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 PM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nEarly afternoon radar mosaic and satellite imagery shows mostly \nclear skies in the eastern two thirds of the UP with an area of \nshowers in Ontonagon County as well as the MN Arrowhead, northwest \nWisconsin, and northwest Ontario. Thus far, lightning activity has \nbeen limited to the Thunder Bay to Isle Royale region and west. \nThese showers and thunderstorms have initiated in the pre-frontal, \nisentropic ascent regime ahead of a RAP-analyzed 1008mb low over \nthe Ontario/Manitoba line and associated cold front draped \nsouth of it. Aloft, vertically stacked troughing is present \nmainly in southern Manitoba, with the trough pivoting around a \nsignificant southwestern CONUS ridge. CAMs have trended lower \nwith the availability of instability with the HREF now showing \npeak MUCAPE values around 500 J/kg until Wednesday afternoon. \nWhile low level hodographs are well-curved, deep shear \n(especially speed shear) is particularly lacking). Given that \nthe strongest forcing will be passing over the UP with only \nmodest shear or instability (despite good moisture presence with\nPWATs climbing to 1.25-1.5 inches, well above the \nclimatological norm), severe weather is unlikely through tonight\n(and if it does, it would occur in the far west where the SPC \ngives 5% chances of severe wind and hail). \n\nThe cold front properly pushes through the UP throughout Wednesday. \nDiurnal destabilization occurs in the east ahead of the front with \naround 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE for much of the east half by 18Z. This \nwill lead to a redevelopment of showers and thunderstorms which \ncould be particularly strong-to-severe in the far east. Shear is \nsimilar to today with good low level curvature but otherwise very \nlittle speed shear, so the severe threat remains marginal. The pre-\nfrontal long-duration and gusty south to southwest winds will drive \nwaves in excess of 4 ft within 5 miles of the Schoolcraft County \nshoreline, which will increase the risk of dangerous rip currents. A \nBeach Hazards Statement may become necessary in the next couple of \nforecast packages. By the time synoptic precipitation ends in the \neast, chances of 1 inch or more of accumulated rainfall are around \n40-60% for the east and 15-35% chance for the west, though there is \nalso a 20-40% chance that some western locations may see less than a \ntenth of an inch of rain, which would certainly not help the \nincreasing drought conditions in that part of the state.\n\nLingering northerly flow behind the shortwave will allow \ntemperatures aloft to fall to similar temperatures that caused light \nlake effect rain showers yesterday, so some PoPs will linger into \nThursday. While the models differ in the details, the global \ndeterministic suite has come into increasing agreement that the \nridge aloft will shift to the southeast CONUS and bring with it a \nmore potent trough through the Great Lakes in the early portions of \nnext week, which could bring unsettled weather to the UP as soon as \nlate Saturday and Sunday.",
